  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal contract obligations in Oregon totaled $1.6B in FY2026 to date, across 7,206 awards with an average award size of $216K. Obligations fell 25.3% year over year. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer, accounting for $902M of obligations in the period, followed by the Department of Agriculture at $170M and the Department of Transportation at $138M. Ship Building and Repairing is the top industry by dollar value at $578M. The single largest vendor is VIGOR MARINE LLC with $563M in obligations. Other notable vendors include COLLINS ELBIT VISION SYSTEMS, LLC at $70.9M and GE RENEWABLES US LLC at $66.7M. Recent years show higher peaks: FY23 had $2.5B and FY24 had $2.4B, while FY25 recorded $2.1B. Current-year totals are partial because the fiscal year is ongoing, so the FY2026 to date figure reflects obligations recorded so far in the year.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What explains the decline in obligations year over year?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 25.3% year over year. The current FY2026 to date total is $1.6B, which is lower than prior full fiscal years such as FY25 at $2.1B and FY24 at $2.4B. The drop coincides with a shift in the mix of large buyers and projects compared with those peak years. Agencies with the largest shares, like the Department of Defense at $902M, still dominate overall spending."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Where is most of the buying concentrated in Oregon?",
        "body": "Buying is concentrated in a few agencies and industries. The Department of Defense accounts for $902M of obligations, and Ship Building and Repairing leads industries at $578M. A small set of vendors capture large shares, for example VIGOR MARINE LLC with $563M. Other agencies and industries together make up the remaining portion of the $1.6B total."
      }
    ]
  },
